---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FeatureHero from '../../components/FeatureHero.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CtaSection from '../../components/CtaSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../site.config';

const title = 'Backup & restore';
const description = 'Declarative CronJob backups of topic records, consumer offsets, and schemas to S3, Azure Blob, GCS, or PVC. Idempotent point-in-time restore with topic remapping. Continuous validation.';

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is backed up exactly?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Topic records (including headers + timestamps), consumer-group offsets, and optionally Apicurio schemas. Topic configs are reapplied on restore from the spec, not the backup.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Is the backup consistent?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Per-partition consistency is guaranteed. Cross-partition consistency is best-effort — Kafka has no global snapshot. PITR uses producer timestamps to bound the recovery window.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How does restore work into a different cluster?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Set the target clusterRef and an optional topicMap. Restore is a one-shot Job that runs an AdminClient + producer pipeline with idempotent semantics — re-running the same Job is a no-op.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can backups be encrypted?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes. The underlying object store handles encryption-at-rest (SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, CMEK). Client-side encryption via an envelope key is on the roadmap.' } },
  ],
};

const backupYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: KafkaBackup
metadata:
  name: prod-nightly
spec:
  clusterRef: prod
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"              # 02:00 UTC daily
  destination: s3://kafka-backups/prod
  includeSchemas: true
  topicSelectors:
    - name: orders
    - name: invoices
    - pattern: "events.*"
  retention:
    days: 30
    minBackups: 7
  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "1Gi"
`;

const restoreYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: KafkaRestore
metadata:
  name: orders-rollback
spec:
  source: s3://kafka-backups/prod/2026-05-22T02-00-00Z
  targetClusterRef: prod
  pointInTime: 2026-05-22T05:14:00Z   # records produced before this timestamp
  topicMap:
    orders: orders-restored             # remap on restore
  conflictPolicy: SKIP_EXISTING         # or OVERWRITE / FAIL
  includeOffsets: true
  includeSchemas: true
`;

const validationYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: KafkaBackupValidation
metadata:
  name: prod-validation
spec:
  backupRef: prod-nightly
  schedule: "0 4 * * *"              # 04:00 UTC daily
  checks:
    - manifestIntegrity              # checksum + envelope
    - partitionCompleteness          # every offset accounted for
    - schemaResolution               # every record's schema is fetchable
`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={faqSchema}>
  <FeatureHero
    eyebrow="Backup & restore"
    title="Declarative backup. Real point-in-time restore."
    lede="A CronJob streams your topics, offsets, and schemas to the object store of your choice. A one-shot Job restores any moment in the retention window into the same cluster — or a different one — idempotently."
    meta={['S3 · Azure · GCS · PVC', 'point-in-time recovery', 'continuous validation', 'idempotent restore']}
  />

  <section class="container-site py-16">
    <Prose>
      <h2 id="problem">Kafka backup is the gap nobody fills.</h2>
      <p>
        Most teams treat Kafka as a streaming buffer and decline to back it
        up. Then a bad producer ships 30 million corrupt records, or an
        engineer drops a topic, or compliance asks for a 30-day audit trail of
        every order, and there is nothing to recover from.
      </p>
      <p>
        {site.brand} ships backup as a first-class CRD. <code>KafkaBackup</code>
        is a CronJob that streams topic records, consumer-group offsets, and
        (optionally) Apicurio schemas to S3, Azure Blob, GCS, or a PVC.
        <code>KafkaRestore</code> is the inverse — an idempotent one-shot
        restore Job with point-in-time recovery and topic remapping.
      </p>

      <h2 id="backup">Scheduled backup.</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={backupYaml} lang="yaml" filename="kafka-backup.yaml" />
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <p>
        The operator generates a <code>CronJob</code> that wraps the
        open-source <a href="https://github.com/osodevops/kafka-backup">osodevops/kafka-backup</a>
        tool — compiled from source onto a Red Hat UBI base image. Each run
        emits a manifest, per-partition record files, an offset snapshot, and
        a schema export. Retention is enforced by the operator, not the
        object store.
      </p>

      <h2 id="restore">Point-in-time restore.</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={restoreYaml} lang="yaml" filename="kafka-restore.yaml" />
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <p>
        The restore Job rebuilds topic configs (idempotent), streams records
        with original timestamps and headers, and stops at the
        <code>pointInTime</code> mark. <code>topicMap</code> lets you restore
        next to existing topics for staged comparison;
        <code>conflictPolicy</code> controls behaviour when target offsets
        already exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Re-running the same <code>KafkaRestore</code> CR is a no-op — every
        write carries an idempotency key. Restores into a different
        Kubernetes cluster work exactly the same way; just point
        <code>targetClusterRef</code> at a different <code>KafkaCluster</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="validation">Continuous validation.</h2>
      <p>
        Untested backups are not backups. <code>KafkaBackupValidation</code>
        is a parallel CronJob that fetches the latest manifest, verifies
        checksums and partition completeness, and (optionally) resolves every
        record's schema against the live registry.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={validationYaml} lang="yaml" filename="kafka-backup-validation.yaml" />
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose">
      <Callout title="vs. Strimzi / MSK">
        <p>
          Strimzi ships no first-party backup. The community answer is "use
          MirrorMaker to a backup cluster" — which costs another Kafka.
        </p>
        <p>
          AWS MSK provides snapshot-style backups, but no PITR, no topic
          remapping, no cross-cluster restore, and no schema export.
        </p>
        <p>
          {site.brand} treats backup as YAML.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaSection />
</Base>
